11. **Projects tags** — "either keep the pills for tags like original, or completely remove the tags and pills altogether. not like the current situation, tags without pills." Plus "If there is an favicon, icon, logo available for a project, then use it instead of our generic logos, if not, keep like current." ✅ DONE
12. **Blog(s) home section** — remove the "notes on coding agents..." subline; decide between chan's thumbnail/square card style vs. our simpler row style — "in simplest terms, it will be like rectangle, not square (current/original scenario). this needs a proper rethink on how to do it, u have to figure out and think fundamentally which is the better approach and go with that." ✅ DONE
13. **Footer** — "change it to dhruv2mars instead of dhruv sharma"; rethink how many socials appear in the "say hi" row and the footer (user has more social profiles available); "make sure that rss feed. rss feature is working perfectly as it should. i havent used/tried rss ever, so i dont know how it works or how to use it. so take care of this." ✅ DONE
14. **dhruv2mars logotype graphic** — "currently the dhruv2mars graphics is generic and not like chan's. see the original repo and see what and how its been implemented, i want that exact behavior and feel." ⬅ IN PROGRESS
15. **/blog page** — "no subheading section. redo (after review) the whole page comparing it with chan's"; apply the same list-style decision chosen for the home blog section; decide whether the footer should appear on blog/other pages ("whats the standard practice here? follow that"); "there has to be the searchbar as well, since the original has it." ⬅ PENDING
16. **Article/post page** — "review and redo it completely, exactly like the original. such as the copy page, share, next/previous, etc. everything like how it is in the original chan's repo." ⬅ PENDING
17. **/projects page** — "it should follow whatever has been decided and implemented upon in the blogs page, with everything." ⬅ PENDING

**Standing constraints (global CLAUDE.md / environment / hooks):**
- Define "done" as a self-verifiable outcome before starting.
- Always strive for concise, simple solutions; propose the simpler way.
- Branch per task, open a draft PR first, delete the branch after merge, small conventional commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`).
- Use skills whenever the task matches its purpose.
- Always use `bun` by default.
- Use the `rtk` CLI proxy (hook-rewritten transparently).
- **SECURITY: The user's email `dhruv.sharma10102005@gmail.com` is to be used only to identify the user (authorship, attribution, filtering their own work). Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.**
- A SessionStart hook sets **CAVEMAN ULTRA mode always on** for user-facing communication (terse, drop articles/filler, abbreviate prose words, arrows for causality; code/commits/PRs written normally; drop caveman for security warnings and irreversible-action confirmations).
- A bypass-permissions system reminder instructs: prefer the Bash tool (cat/head/sed/grep/find/heredocs/short scripts) over Read/Edit/Write wherever Bash can do the job.
- Do not call the Agent tool or use Workflows/deep-research unless the user requests it.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Next.js 16 App Router, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4 (`@utility`, `@custom-variant`, `@theme inline`, bare-value utilities like `duration-350`), Motion (`motion/react`), `next-themes`, `next-mdx-remote`, `rehype-sanitize`, `sugar-high`, Geist Sans/Mono, IBM Plex Serif + Caveat, Vercel Analytics/Speed Insights/Blob.
   - Bun for everything: `bunx next dev`, `bun test`, `bunx next typegen && bunx tsc --noEmit`, `bun run lint` (→ `bunx eslint .`, eslint 9 + eslint-config-next 16).
   - `tsconfig.json` **excludes `**/*.test.ts`** from typechecking — test fixtures are not type-verified by `tsc`.
   - Panel layout system: `Panel`, `PanelHeader`, `PanelTitle`, `PanelTitleSup`, `PanelDescription`, `PanelContent`; utilities `screen-line-top/bottom`, `stripe-divider`, `border-line`, `link`, `link-underline`, `extend-touch-target`, `diagonal-stripes`, `typeset`, `typeset-description`.
   - `grid-template-columns: 1fr → 0fr` collapse trick (used by `.project-details` and the header wordmark morph).
   - `useScroll` + `useMotionValueEvent` with hysteresis for scroll-linked state.
   - `AnimatePresence mode="wait"` + variants (y/opacity/blur) for text flipping; remounting on key restarts a CSS `animation: shimmer-sweep 1.5s linear 1`.
   - Web Speech API (`SpeechSynthesisUtterance`, `speechSynthesis.cancel()`) with a respelling + audio-file slot.
   - `useSyncExternalStore(subscribeNever, snapshot, () => false)` as the lint-clean way to read a browser capability once after hydration without `setState` in an effect (avoids `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect`).
   - CSS `mask-image` + `background: currentColor` to draw an arbitrary SVG as a monochrome silhouette in theme ink (used for project logos).
   - Listbox/combobox ARIA (`aria-activedescendant`, `role="option"`, `role="group"` + `aria-labelledby`), custom key-repeat for grid traversal.
   - Reference (chanhdai) `ContributionGraph` metrics: `blockSize=12`, `blockMargin=2`, `blockRadius=0`, `fontSize=14`, `LABEL_MARGIN=8`, `labelHeight = fontSize + LABEL_MARGIN` (22px), `width = weeks.length * (blockSize + blockMargin) - blockMargin`. Its calendar wrapper is `no-scrollbar max-w-full scroll-fade-x overflow-x-auto overflow-y-hidden` — so the reference also horizontally scrolls at md:max-w-3xl.
   - RSS 2.0 correctness: `xmlns:atom`, `<atom:link rel="self">`, `<lastBuildDate>`, `<language>`, `<guid isPermaLink="true">`; Next.js `metadata.alternates.types` array form `[{url, title}]` for the auto-discovery `<link rel="alternate">`.

3. Files and Code Sections:

   **`components/activity-grid.tsx`** (item 10 second half) — removed the measured cell size entirely.
   - Deleted: `const CELL_GAP = 2; const CELL_MIN = 12; const CELL_MAX = 18;`, the `const [cellSize, setCellSize] = useState(CELL_MIN)` state, and the whole ResizeObserver `measure()` effect.
   - Scroll effect now:
     ```tsx
     // The year is wider than a phone and, at 53 columns, a shade wider than the
     // rail on a desktop too. The end of it is the part worth opening on — today
     // is the last column. A rail that is short by only a few pixels is left where
     // it is: scrolling it would push the first week under the edge fade for
     // nothing.
     useEffect(() => {
       const strip = scrollRef.current;
       if (!strip) return;
       const overflow = strip.scrollWidth - strip.clientWidth;
       if (overflow > 64) strip.scrollLeft = strip.scrollWidth;
     }, [weeks]);
     ```
   - Style prop now `style={{ "--weeks": weeks.length } as CSSProperties}`.
   - Untouched: `REPEAT_DELAY_MS = 300`, `REPEAT_INTERVAL_MS = 55`, `STEP_BY_KEY`, arrow-key traversal, `Fig. 2.` figcaption with the less/more legend.

   **`app/globals.css`** (activity block, ~line 392+):
   ```css
   /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
      Activity mosaic. Square blocks on a 2px gutter, no corner rounding — the
      grid is a measuring instrument, so its cells are not decorated. Intensity
      is carried by the neutral foreground at five opacities.

      Every measurement here is the reference's: a 12px block on a 2px gutter,
      under a 22px label band. They are not arbitrary — a rolling year at that
      size is a fixed 740px plate, the same shape every month of every year, which
      is the whole point of drawing a habit as a calendar. A cell that grew to
      fill the rail would make the same year a different picture each visit.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

   .activity-grid {
     --cell-size: 12px;
     --cell-gap: 2px;
     --label-size: 12px;
     display: grid;
     /* A length, not a fraction. A `1fr` track lets the browser round one week
        to 12px and the next to 13, which on a grid of squares reads as a wobble
        in every row. */
     grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--weeks, 53), var(--cell-size));
     gap: var(--cell-gap);
     outline: none;
     padding-inline: 3px;
   }

   .activity-col {
     position: relative;
     display: grid;
     grid-template-rows: repeat(7, var(--cell-size));
     gap: var(--cell-gap);
     /* The label band: the type size plus the 8px of air the reference leaves
        under it, so the months sit above the plate rather than on it. */
     padding-top: calc(var(--label-size) + 8px);
   }

   .activity-month {
     position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0;
     font-family: var(--font-mono);
     font-size: var(--label-size);
     line-height: 1;
     color: var(--muted-foreground);
     white-space: nowrap;
     pointer-events: none;
   }
   ```

   **`lib/ai-activity.ts`** — the window is now counted in whole weeks off the current week:
   ```ts
   export const ACTIVITY_WEEKS = 52;

   /**
    * The Sunday that opens the window: this week's Sunday, then fifty-two Sundays
    * back from it.
    *
    * Counting whole weeks off the current week — rather than counting 364 days
    * back from today and rounding — is what makes the plate exactly 53 columns on
    * every day of every week. Counting days puts a Saturday at exactly 52 columns
    * and every other day at 53, and a figure that loses a column once a week is a
    * figure that moves under the Visitor.
    */
   export function activityWindowStart(
     today: string,
     weeks = ACTIVITY_WEEKS,
   ): string {
     return shiftYmd(today, -(weekdayIndex(today) + weeks * 7));
   }
   ```
   Also present from earlier: `ActivityTotals`, `sumTrailing`, `totals` on `AiActivity`.

   **`lib/ai-activity.test.ts`** — the `describe("window")` block replaced wholesale:
   ```ts
   describe("window", () => {
     test("opens on a Sunday, so every column is a whole week", () => {
       // 2026-08-19 is a Wednesday; its week opens on the 16th, and 52 Sundays
       // before that is 2025-08-17.
       expect(activityWindowStart("2026-08-19")).toBe("2025-08-17");
       expect(weekdayIndex(activityWindowStart("2026-08-19"))).toBe(0);
     });

     test("is the same width whatever day it is asked on", () => {
       const widths = new Set(
         Array.from({ length: 14 }, (_, i) => {
           const today = shiftYmd("2026-08-19", i);
           const start = activityWindowStart(today);
           return Math.round(
             (Date.parse(`${today}T00:00:00Z`) - Date.parse(`${start}T00:00:00Z`)) /
               86_400_000,
           );
         }),
       );
       // 52 whole weeks plus however much of this one has happened — 53 columns
       // on every day of the week, including Saturday.
       for (const days of widths) {
         expect(Math.ceil((days + 1) / 7)).toBe(ACTIVITY_WEEKS + 1);
       }
     });

     test("a shorter window is still Sunday-aligned", () => {
       // Wednesday 2026-08-19 sits in the week that opened on the 16th; four
       // Sundays before that is 2026-07-19.
       expect(activityWindowStart("2026-08-19", 4)).toBe("2026-07-19");
     });
   });
   ```
