7. **TTS name pronunciation** — speaker icon by the name, "in the most correct way possible". ✅ DONE (`869de5b`)
8. **Multiple flipping sublines with gradient sweep** ✅ DONE (`869de5b`)
9. **Delhi/timezone overview section** — decide whether it should exist. ✅ DONE (`62c3aa4`, folded into the socials panel)
10. **Token activity section** — replace "10B tokens" subline with 4 stats (total / 30 days / 7 days / 24hrs); redo the cells/map to match the original's polish; keep arrow-key cell movement. ✅ DONE (`81f332e`)
11. **Projects tags** — pills like the original or no tags at all; use real project favicons/logos where available. ✅ DONE (`3d6e54e`)
12. **Blog(s) home section** — remove the "notes on coding agents..." subline; decide card vs. row style from first principles. ✅ DONE (`775c55f`, rows)
13. **Footer** — "dhruv2mars" not "dhruv sharma"; rethink socials in "say hi" row vs. footer; "make sure that rss feed. rss feature is working perfectly as it should. i havent used/tried rss ever, so i dont know how it works or how to use it. so take care of this." ✅ DONE (`7f4e812`)
14. **dhruv2mars logotype graphic** — "currently the dhruv2mars graphics is generic and not like chan's. see the original repo and see what and how its been implemented, i want that exact behavior and feel." ✅ DONE this session (`bc9be66`)
15. **/blog page** — "no subheading section. redo (after review) the whole page comparing it with chan's"; same list style as home; decide whether the footer appears on inner pages ("whats the standard practice here? follow that"); "there has to be the searchbar as well, since the original has it." ✅ DONE this session (`904a966`)
16. **Article/post page** — "review and redo it completely, exactly like the original. such as the copy page, share, next/previous, etc. everything like how it is in the original chan's repo." ⬅ IN PROGRESS (research done, no code written)
17. **/projects page** — "it should follow whatever has been decided and implemented upon in the blogs page, with everything." ⬅ PENDING

**Standing constraints (global CLAUDE.md / environment / hooks):**
- Define "done" as a self-verifiable outcome before starting.
- Always strive for concise, simple solutions; propose the simpler way.
- Branch per task, open a draft PR first, delete the branch after merge, small conventional commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`).
- Use skills whenever the task matches its purpose.
- Always use `bun` by default.
- Use the `rtk` CLI proxy (hook-rewritten transparently).
- **SECURITY: The user's email `dhruv.sharma10102005@gmail.com` is to be used only to identify the user (authorship, attribution, filtering their own work). Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.**
- A SessionStart hook sets **CAVEMAN ULTRA mode always on** for user-facing communication (terse, drop articles/filler, abbreviate prose words, arrows for causality; code/commits/PRs written normally; drop caveman for security warnings and irreversible-action confirmations).
- A bypass-permissions system reminder instructs: prefer the Bash tool (cat/head/sed/grep/find/heredocs/short scripts) over Read/Edit/Write wherever Bash can do the job.
- Do not call the Agent tool or use Workflows/deep-research unless the user requests it.
- Three MCP servers (claude.ai AngelList, Ashby, Indeed) need OAuth authorization and are unavailable in this non-interactive session; do not ask the user for tokens/codes.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Next.js 16.2.10 (Turbopack) App Router, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4 (`@theme inline`, `@utility`, arbitrary/bare-value utilities), Motion v12 (`motion/react`), `next-themes`, `next-mdx-remote/rsc`, `rehype-sanitize`, `sugar-high`, Geist Sans/Mono, IBM Plex Serif + Caveat, Vercel Analytics/Speed Insights.
   - Bun: `bunx next dev`, `bun test`, `bunx tsc --noEmit`, `bun run lint` (→ `bunx eslint .`).
   - `tsconfig.json` excludes `**/*.test.ts` from typechecking.
   - Panel system: `Panel`, `PanelHeader`, `PanelTitle`, `PanelTitleSup`, `PanelDescription`, `PanelContent`; utilities `screen-line-top/bottom`, `stripe-divider`, `border-line`, `link-underline`, `extend-touch-target`, `typeset`, `typeset-description`, `typeset-timescale`.
   - Panel headers are tight: `PanelHeader` is `screen-line-bottom px-4` with **zero vertical padding**; `PanelTitle` is `font-heading text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight text-balance` with line-height 36px, so a header band is exactly 36px tall.
   - One global focus ring: `:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ring); outline-offset: 2px; }` — components must not add their own ring, and must not set `outline-none`.
   - `--color-line`, `--color-border`, `--color-input`, `--color-ring` are all in `@theme inline`, so `stroke-line`, `border-border`, `dark:bg-input/30` etc. all generate.
   - Motion's frame loop is rAF-driven: in a hidden browser tab, `requestAnimationFrame` never fires, so `useTransform`/`useSpring` values never update even though `MotionValue.set()` works. This makes headless verification of motion impossible in the preview tab.
   - `useId()` in React 19 returns characters that are legal in an HTML id but illegal in a CSS selector; `fill="url(#…)"` is parsed as a selector, so ids must be sanitized.
   - `lib/blog.ts` imports `node:fs` → anything importing a *value* from it is server-only. Types are erased, so a types-only module is client-safe.
   - Reference blog/doc patterns: `PageHeading`/`PageHeadingTagline`/`PageHeadingTitle`, `PostSearchInput` (nuqs `useQueryState("q")`), `useFilteredPosts` (normalize = lowercase + strip spaces), `findNeighbour`, `DocKeyboardShortcuts` (react-hotkeys-hook ArrowLeft/ArrowRight, bailing on `event.defaultPrevented`), `LLMCopyButtonWithViewOptions`, `DocShareMenu`, `TOCInline` + `TOCMinimap`.

3. Files and Code Sections:

   **`components/site-wordmark.tsx`** (item 14 — REWRITTEN, now a client component). Full current content:
   ```tsx
   "use client";

   import {
     motion,
     useMotionValue,
     useReducedMotion,
     useSpring,
     useTransform,
   } from "motion/react";
   import { useId, type PointerEvent as ReactPointerEvent } from "react";

   /**
    * The name, drawn rather than set: a 7-row bitmap on the same square cell the
    * activity grid uses, so the page closes on the unit it opened with.
    *
    * Two passes over one cell set. The first is a hairline outline that is always
    * there — the name is present on the page whether or not anyone touches it.
    * The second fills those same cells through a gradient that is transparent
    * until 62.5% of its own length and solid after, and the gradient's near end is
    * tied to the pointer. So the ink is not painted *at* the cursor; it runs away
    * from it, and moving across the mark tips a wash of it from one side of the
    * name to the other.
    *
    * At rest the near end sits at the horizontal middle, which makes the gradient
    * vertical: the bottom of the letters is inked and the tops fade to outline, so
    * the signature reads as filling up rather than as waiting for a mouse. A
    * phone, which never sends a pointer, gets that resting state and nothing else
    * — which is the point of choosing a resting state that stands on its own.
    *
    * The cells are declared once in `<defs>` and drawn twice with `<use>`, so the
    * markup carries ~140 rects rather than ~280.
    */
   const GLYPHS: Readonly<Record<string, readonly string[]>> = {
     a: [".....", ".....", ".###.", "....#", ".####", "#...#", ".####"],
     d: ["....#", "....#", ".####", "#...#", "#...#", "#...#", ".####"],
     h: ["#....", "#....", "#.##.", "##..#", "#...#", "#...#", "#...#"],
     m: [".....", ".....", "##.##", "#.#.#", "#.#.#", "#.#.#", "#.#.#"],
     r: [".....", ".....", "#.##.", "##..#", "#....", "#....", "#...."],
     s: [".....", ".....", ".####", "#....", ".###.", "....#", "####."],
     u: [".....", ".....", "#...#", "#...#", "#...#", "#..##", ".##.#"],
     v: [".....", ".....", "#...#", "#...#", "#...#", ".#.#.", "..#.."],
     "2": [".###.", "#...#", "....#", "...#.", "..#..", ".#...", "#####"],
   };

   const ROWS = 7;
   /** One blank column between letters — the grid's own gutter, not kerning. */
   const TRACKING = 1;

   /**
    * Where the wash stops being nothing and starts being ink, measured along the
    * gradient rather than across the mark. The reference's number, and it is the
    * one that makes the resting state work: 0.625 of seven rows puts the tide line
    * between the fifth and sixth, which is where these letterforms have their
    * waist.
    */
   const INK_OFFSET = 0.625;

   /** Heavy enough that the wash lags the pointer instead of tracking it. */
   const SPRING = { stiffness: 150, damping: 25 } as const;

   function layout(text: string) {
     const cells: { x: number; y: number }[] = [];
     let cursor = 0;

     for (const char of text) {
       const glyph = GLYPHS[char];
       if (!glyph) continue;
       for (let y = 0; y < glyph.length; y++) {
         for (let x = 0; x < glyph[y].length; x++) {
           if (glyph[y][x] === "#") cells.push({ x: cursor + x, y });
         }
       }
       cursor += glyph[0].length + TRACKING;
     }

     return { cells, width: Math.max(cursor - TRACKING, 0) };
   }

   export function SiteWordmark({
     text,
     className,
   }: {
     text: string;
     className?: string;
   }) {
     // `useId` returns a value fenced in characters that are legal in an HTML id
     // but not in a CSS selector, and `fill="url(#…)"` is read as one. Stripping
     // them back to the counter keeps the uniqueness and loses the hazard.
     const id = useId().replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9-]/g, "");
     const cellsId = `${id}-cells`;
     const inkId = `${id}-ink`;

     const { cells, width } = layout(text);
     const reduced = useReducedMotion();

     /** The pointer as a fraction of the mark's width. Centre is the rest. */
     const ratio = useMotionValue(0.5);
     const x1 = useSpring(useTransform(ratio, [0, 1], [0, width]), SPRING);

     const handleMove = (event: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {
       if (reduced) return;
       const box = event.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
       if (box.width === 0) return;
       ratio.set((event.clientX - box.left) / box.width);
     };

     return (
       <div
         onPointerMove={handleMove}
         onPointerLeave={() => ratio.set(0.5)}
         className={className}
       >
         <svg
           aria-hidden
           viewBox={`0 0 ${width} ${ROWS}`}
           className="w-full"
